\easrptitle{Sugar~I: A Hybrid Transformer--JEPA Architecture for Personalizable
Next-Hour Glucose Forecasting from Wearable and Insulin Pump Signals}

\begin{document}

\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Accurate 60-minute glucose forecasting can prevent hypoglycemic episodes and optimize insulin dosing, yet current models treat the task as a pure regression problem without considering whether their predictions are traceable enough to support clinical decisions.
We present Sugar~I, a compact transformer architecture ($\sim$200K--420K parameters) that fuses heterogeneous physiological signals through parallel cross-attention (sensor fusion) and multi-scale self-attention (temporal context at multiple resolutions).
Sugar~I accepts interchangeable covariate sets---heart rate and step counts from wearables, or basal rate, bolus insulin, and carbohydrates from insulin pumps---unified by learnable softmax mixing that lets the model discover each signal's contribution.
On AI-READI (896 participants, four T2DM-spectrum cohorts) and Loop T1DM data, the wearable configuration achieves MARD 8.25\%, approaching the measurement accuracy of CGM sensors themselves ($\sim$10\%).

We further integrate a self-supervised JEPA encoder as an auxiliary cross-attention stream, motivated by the Joint-Embedding Predictive Architecture's promise of separating state, action, and prediction into inspectable components---a prerequisite for biomedical decision support where traceability must come before capability.
The resulting hybrid (Sugar~I + JEPA) reduces MAE by up to 18\% over the pump-only baseline, and linear probing of frozen JEPA embeddings recovers patient identity with 68\% accuracy across 665 individuals, demonstrating their potential as zero-shot patient embeddings.
A personalization study on 15 subjects shows that fine-tuning from 60 days of personal data consistently improves predictions, while shorter windows typical of health-optimization CGM use do not yet suffice.
The ongoing Sugar-Sugar human benchmarking study (ethics-approved, $n > 200$) will provide the first published human accuracy reference for glucose forecasting.
All code and model weights are publicly available.
\end{abstract}

% ================================================================
\section{Introduction}
% ================================================================

Diabetes mellitus affects 537 million adults worldwide and is projected to reach 783 million by 2045~\citep{idf2021atlas}.
The disease spans a wide spectrum of severity: Type~2 diabetes (T2DM) ranges from prediabetes through oral-medication and insulin-dependent stages, while Type~1 diabetes (T1DM) is characterized by complete insulin dependence and large, rapid glucose excursions.
For people with T1DM and insulin-dependent T2DM, accurate glucose forecasting is not a convenience but a safety-critical need---mistimed insulin can cause life-threatening hypoglycemia, and even brief hyperglycemic episodes accelerate long-term complications.
Continuous glucose monitoring (CGM) devices measure interstitial glucose every five minutes, producing dense physiological time series that enable data-driven forecasting~\citep{rodbard2016cgm}.
However, CGMs display \emph{current} glucose; by the time a patient observes a dangerous value, the event is already underway.
Accurate 60-minute forecasting would enable proactive intervention---preventing hypoglycemic episodes, optimizing insulin dosing, and improving time-in-range (the proportion of readings within the clinical target of 70--180~mg/dL).

Beyond insulin-dependent populations, CGM adoption is growing among healthy individuals and those with prediabetes who use sensors for metabolic health optimization---often wearing a sensor for just one or two weeks per year.
For these users, the practical question is whether such a short personal data window is sufficient for a forecasting model to personalize.

CGM sensors measure glucose (and, in some newer devices, ketones), but they do not capture the broader physiological context.
Smartwatches and fitness trackers worn alongside a CGM can provide heart rate (HR) and physical activity (step counts), signals that may carry complementary metabolic and cardiovascular information.
Whether these additional modalities genuinely improve glucose prediction is an open question.
The AI-READI dataset pairs Dexcom~G6 CGM with Fitbit-derived HR and step counts, giving us the opportunity to train both glucose-only and multimodal models and compare them directly.
On the insulin pump side, Loop system data provides basal rate, bolus insulin, and carbohydrate entries---covariates with a more direct pharmacological relationship to glucose dynamics.
The architectural challenge is to fuse these heterogeneous, irregularly sampled signals into a single predictive model that generalizes across the full clinical spectrum---from healthy individuals through prediabetes to insulin-dependent T1DM and T2DM---while remaining personalizable to each user.

Recent work has shown that transformer architectures with cross-sensor attention can generalize across sensor types and populations for wearable biosignals~\citep{luo2024biosignal, lutsker2024wearable}.
\citet{bian2025glumind} proposed GluMind, a multimodal parallel-attention transformer that combines cross-attention for sensor fusion with multi-scale self-attention for capturing temporal dependencies at multiple resolutions, evaluated on the AI-READI dataset.
However, a separate gap persists in evaluation methodology: published models are compared only against other models, with no reference to human performance on the same task.

In this paper, we make the following contributions:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item We present \textbf{Sugar~I}, our implementation and extension of the GluMind architecture, achieving state-of-the-art results on the AI-READI T2DM cohorts (MAE 11.33~mg/dL, MARD 8.25\%) and competitive results on T1DM data.
    \item We extend Sugar~I from wearable covariates (HR, steps) to \textbf{insulin pump signals} (basal, bolus, carbohydrates) via \emph{learnable softmax mixing weights}, and demonstrate that insulin covariates improve T1DM prediction by 3.6\%.
    \item We conduct a systematic \textbf{personalization study} treating the global Sugar~I checkpoint as a foundation model and asking: \emph{what is the minimum viable personal data} for fine-tuning to improve over the global model? Across 15 subjects spanning five clinical groups---from T1DM patients with months of pump data to healthy users with only days of CGM---we find that 30--60 days are needed, and that shorter windows (as typical for health-optimization users who wear a sensor once a year) do not yet suffice.
    \item We introduce \textbf{Sugar-Sugar}, an ethics-approved web-based human benchmarking study ($n > 200$ target, currently recruiting) that will produce the first published human accuracy baseline for glucose forecasting (to be described in a separate publication).
    \item We release all code, preprocessing pipelines (supporting 9 CGM device formats across 50+ public datasets), and model weights as open-source resources.
\end{enumerate}

% ================================================================
\section{Related Work}
% ================================================================

\paragraph{Classical and shallow approaches.}
ARIMA models and their exogenous extensions (ARX) provide interpretable baselines for blood glucose forecasting but struggle with the nonlinear, non-stationary dynamics of glucose regulation~\citep{bian2025glumind}.
Shallow feedforward networks improve on linear methods but cannot model complex temporal dependencies.

\paragraph{Recurrent models.}
LSTMs and GRUs have been widely applied to glucose prediction, with personalized multitask architectures showing promise for T1DM~\citep{li2019lstm, zhu2022personalized}.
However, recurrent models face difficulties with long-range dependencies, varying sampling rates, and catastrophic forgetting during sequential fine-tuning across populations.

\paragraph{Transformer-based models.}
Transformers have emerged as a powerful alternative for time-series forecasting~\citep{vaswani2017attention}.
The Informer~\citep{zhou2021informer} and Temporal Fusion Transformer~\citep{lim2021tft} address long-sequence efficiency and interpretability, respectively.
For glucose specifically, GluFormer~\citep{sergazinov2023gluformer} incorporates hierarchical attention and uncertainty quantification.
AttenGluco~\citep{zhu2023attengluco} introduced cross-attention for multimodal glucose forecasting but employed a \emph{sequential} configuration.
N-HiTS~\citep{challu2023nhits} uses hierarchical interpolation for multi-horizon forecasting and serves as a strong non-transformer baseline.
GluMind~\citep{bian2025glumind} proposed running cross-attention and multi-scale attention in \emph{parallel} and introduced Learning without Forgetting (LwF)~\citep{li2017lwf} for continual learning across cohorts, evaluated on AI-READI.

\paragraph{Self-supervised and foundation models.}
Recent work has explored self-supervised pretraining for CGM data.
CGM-JEPA~\citep{cgmjepa2025} applies joint-embedding predictive architecture to glucose time series.
Foundation models for wearable biosignals~\citep{luo2024biosignal, lutsker2024wearable} demonstrate that cross-sensor attention can generalize across devices and populations.

\paragraph{Benchmarks.}
Several recent efforts have standardized glucose forecasting evaluation.
GlucoBench~\citep{glucobench2024} curated 13 public CGM datasets with unified prediction benchmarks.
MetaboNet-Bench~\citep{jeffries2026metabonetbench} extends this to multi-modal T1DM data (glucose, insulin, carbohydrates) in an extensible open-source framework.
EventGlucoseBench~\citep{luo2026eventglucose} introduces event-aware sampling centered on meals, medications, and exercise, with probabilistic evaluation via CRPS across 18 models.
Despite this progress, a critical evaluation gap remains: no published study compares model predictions against human accuracy on the same glucose forecasting task.

% ================================================================
\section{Method}
% ================================================================

\subsection{Problem formulation}

Given a set of $n$ sensor-derived time series $\mathbf{X} = [\mathbf{x}_1, \ldots, \mathbf{x}_n]$ sampled over a lookback window of $T$ steps, we predict future glucose values $\hat{\mathbf{x}}_g = [x_{g,T+1}, \ldots, x_{g,T+H}]^\top$ over a horizon of $H$ steps.
In our primary configuration, $n = 3$ (glucose, heart rate, step count), $T = 80$ (400 minutes at 5-minute sampling), and $H = 12$ (60 minutes).

\subsection{Sugar~I architecture}

Sugar~I builds on the GluMind framework~\citep{bian2025glumind} with a parallel dual-attention design.
Each of the $n$ input channels is independently projected from scalar dimension to $d_\text{model}$ via a learned linear embedding, then augmented with sinusoidal positional encoding.
The resulting representations are processed by a stack of $B$ identical \texttt{ParallelBlocks}, each containing two sub-networks that execute simultaneously (Figure~\ref{fig:sugar_i_arch}):

\paragraph{Cross-attention block (sensor fusion).}
Glucose embeddings serve as queries $\mathbf{Q}$; each auxiliary modality (HR, steps) independently provides keys $\mathbf{K}_i$ and values $\mathbf{V}_i$ through separate multi-head attention modules:
\begin{equation}
    \mathbf{C}_i = \text{MultiHead}(\mathbf{Q}_\text{gluc}, \mathbf{K}_i, \mathbf{V}_i), \quad i \in \{1, \ldots, n-1\}
\end{equation}
The outputs are averaged with equal weights ($1/(n-1)$ each), followed by LayerNorm, a feedforward network (Linear $\to$ GELU $\to$ Dropout $\to$ Linear), and residual connections.
This design allows the model to learn \emph{when} and \emph{how much} each auxiliary signal informs glucose dynamics---for example, detecting that a heart rate spike after a meal predicts a subsequent glucose rise.

\paragraph{Multi-scale attention block (temporal context).}
Self-attention is applied to the glucose stream at three temporal resolutions: full resolution (downsampling factor DS=1), half resolution (DS=2 via average pooling), and quarter resolution (DS=4 via average pooling).
Each resolution has its own multi-head attention module.
Downsampled representations are processed independently, then upsampled via nearest-neighbor interpolation back to full resolution, and summed:
\begin{equation}
    \mathbf{M} = \sum_{s \in \{1,2,4\}} \text{Upsample}\bigl(\text{MultiHead}_s(\mathbf{G}_s, \mathbf{G}_s, \mathbf{G}_s)\bigr)
\end{equation}
where $\mathbf{G}_s$ denotes the glucose representation at downsampling factor $s$.
This multi-scale design captures rapid glucose fluctuations (5-minute scale), meal and activity responses (10-minute scale), and longer metabolic trends (20-minute scale) simultaneously.

\paragraph{Fusion and output.}
The cross-attention output $\mathbf{C}$ and multi-scale output $\mathbf{M}$ are summed and fused through a LayerNorm.
After the final block, the representation is flattened and passed through a two-layer MLP ($d_\text{model} \times T \to d_\text{model} \to H$) with GELU activation to produce the $H$-step forecast.

\begin{figure}[ht]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.85\textwidth]{fig_architecture.png}
\caption{Sugar~I architecture. Three sensor streams (CGM, heart rate, steps) are independently embedded, then processed by parallel cross-attention (sensor fusion) and multi-scale self-attention (temporal context at DS=1, 2, 4) blocks, repeated $\times 3$. The fused representation is decoded by an MLP into 12 glucose predictions (60 minutes).}
\label{fig:sugar_i_arch}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Hyperparameters.}
The default configuration uses $d_\text{model} = 32$, $n_\text{heads} = 4$, $B = 3$ blocks, $\text{ff\_units} = 128$, and dropout = 0.1, yielding approximately \textbf{197K parameters}---compact enough for edge deployment on wearable devices.
Training uses AdamW~\citep{loshchilov2019adamw} with learning rate $10^{-3}$, weight decay $10^{-4}$, batch size 4096, bf16 mixed precision~\citep{micikevicius2018mixed}, and early stopping with patience 20.

\subsection{Insulin and carbohydrate extension}

For insulin pump data, the auxiliary covariates change from (HR, steps) to (basal rate, bolus insulin, carbohydrates), yielding $n = 4$ input channels with $T = 128$ steps.
The key architectural innovation is replacing the fixed-weight averaging of cross-attention outputs with \textbf{learnable softmax mixing}:
\begin{equation}
    \mathbf{C} = \sum_{i=1}^{3} w_i \cdot \mathbf{C}_i, \quad \text{where} \quad w_i = \frac{\exp(\alpha_i)}{\sum_{j=1}^{3} \exp(\alpha_j)}
\end{equation}
and $\boldsymbol{\alpha} = (\alpha_1, \alpha_2, \alpha_3)$ are learned parameters initialized to zero (equal mixing).
This allows the model to autonomously discover that bolus insulin events (sparse but highly informative) should contribute differently from basal rate (continuous background signal).
The production pump configuration uses $n_\text{heads} = 8$ and $B = 5$ blocks.

\subsection{JEPA auxiliary stream: toward a glucose world model}

The pump configuration is further extended with a fourth cross-attention stream derived from a pretrained JEPA encoder based on CGM-JEPA~\citep{cgmjepa2025}.

\paragraph{Why JEPA.}
The Joint-Embedding Predictive Architecture, popularized by \citet{lecun2022jepa}, was proposed as an alternative to autoregressive generative models.
Its central promise is that the current state, a proposed action, and the predicted future state can be represented as distinct, inspectable parts of the model.
This does not make predictions safe by default; it gives us a concrete safety claim to test.
For biomedical decision support, traceability must come before capability: a plausible but untraceable prediction cannot safely support a clinical decision.
Our JEPA experiment tests how far this requirement can be built into a physiological world model.
The encoder receives recent glucose history, and the resulting latent state enters Sugar~I's cross-attention alongside explicit pharmacological signals (insulin, carbohydrates), producing a trajectory that can be audited at the embedding level.

\paragraph{JEPA encoder training.}
We train our own JEPA encoders on the unified Loop + AI-READI dataset (glucose only, no covariates), holding out a random 5\% subset for validation.
In addition to the exponential moving average (EMA) teacher--student regularization from CGM-JEPA, we add a variance penalty inspired by VICReg~\citep{bardes2022vicreg}:
\begin{equation}
    L = \underbrace{\operatorname{SmoothL1}(\hat{z}, z)}_{\text{prediction loss}} + \lambda \underbrace{\frac{1}{E} \sum_{j=1}^{E} \operatorname{ReLU}\!\left(\sigma_{\text{target}} - \sigma_{c,j}\right)}_{\text{variance penalty}}
\end{equation}
where $E$ is the embedding dimensionality and $\sigma_{c,j}$ is the standard deviation of context-block representations along dimension $j$.
This modification increases the number of informative dimensions and reduces representation collapse.

To evaluate the effect of temporal context length, we train five encoder variants with input windows from 128 to 2016 steps (10.7 hours to 7 days), as summarized in Table~\ref{tab:jepa_variants}.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{JEPA encoder variants evaluated.}
\label{tab:jepa_variants}
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\toprule
\textbf{Encoder} & \textbf{Embed dim} & \textbf{Context size} \\
\midrule
jepa-128-64 & 64 & 128 (10.7\,h) \\
jepa-128 & 96 & 128 (10.7\,h) \\
jepa-288 & 96 & 288 (1\,d) \\
jepa-864 & 96 & 864 (3\,d) \\
jepa-2016 & 96 & 2016 (7\,d) \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Integration with Sugar~I.}
The pretrained JEPA encoder is added as an additional covariate branch, analogous to bolus, basal, and carbohydrate inputs.
The JEPA embeddings are layer-normalized and linearly projected from 96 to $d_\text{model} = 32$ dimensions before entering the multi-head cross-attention block.
When the JEPA encoder requires $m$ input steps and Sugar~I requires $n < m$ steps, training windows contain $m$ points: the full sequence feeds the JEPA encoder while only the final $n$ points feed the main branches.
The 4-way learnable softmax mixing (Eq.~3 extended with a fourth term) integrates pharmacological covariates with the self-supervised glucose representation.
During training with the JEPA stream, encoder weights are \emph{not} frozen but updated with a lower learning rate ($4 \times 10^{-5}$ vs.\ $4 \times 10^{-4}$ for the rest of the model).

\begin{figure}[ht]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.8\textwidth]{sugar_jepa.png}
\caption{Sugar~I + JEPA architecture. The pump configuration is augmented with a JEPA-based auxiliary branch whose embeddings enter via a fourth cross-attention stream with learnable softmax mixing.}
\label{fig:sugar_jepa}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{JEPA as patient embedding.}
A key practical challenge in glucose prediction is adaptation to individual patients.
Standard personalization requires accumulating sufficient patient-specific data for fine-tuning, during which no personalized model is available.
We hypothesize that JEPA representations can serve as patient embeddings, capturing enough patient-specific information for \emph{zero-shot} personalization---the observation period for a representative JEPA embedding is substantially shorter than what fine-tuning requires.
To test this, we train a dense classification head on top of frozen JEPA encoders for a patient identification task on held-out patients (see Section~\ref{sec:jepa_results}).

\subsection{Continual learning with LwF}

Following \citet{bian2025glumind}, we support continual training across cohorts (Healthy $\to$ Pre-T2DM $\to$ Oral-T2DM $\to$ Insulin-T2DM) using Learning without Forgetting~\citep{li2017lwf}:
\begin{equation}
    \mathcal{L} = (1 - \lambda)\,\mathcal{L}_\text{task} + \lambda\,\mathcal{L}_\text{distill}
\end{equation}
where $\mathcal{L}_\text{distill}$ is the MSE between the current model's predictions and those of a frozen teacher snapshot from the previous cohort.
We find $\lambda = 0.3$ provides the best balance between plasticity and stability, with only $\sim$0.14~mg/dL MAE trade-off versus global training.

\subsection{Personalization}

We treat the globally trained Sugar~I (pump) checkpoint as a \emph{foundation model} and ask: what is the minimum amount of personal data required for fine-tuning to improve over the global model?
This question is practically important across the clinical spectrum.
T1DM and insulin-dependent T2DM patients accumulate months of continuous data from their pumps and CGMs, but healthy individuals and those with prediabetes who use CGM for metabolic health optimization may wear a sensor for only one or two weeks per year.

Given the global checkpoint, personal fine-tuning uses the individual's chronological CGM and pump data (last 25\% test, 15\% of remainder validation, rest train), sweeping train budgets from 1 to 345 days.
Critically, MinMax scalers are \emph{not} refitted on personal data but reused from the global checkpoint's \texttt{scalers.json}, preserving the pretrained input distribution.
The locked recipe is: plain fine-tune ($\lambda = 0$), learning rate $2 \times 10^{-4}$, weight decay $3 \times 10^{-5}$, train window stride 6 (30-minute spacing), bf16, early stopping with patience 3.

% ================================================================
\section{Experimental Setup}
% ================================================================

\subsection{Datasets}

\paragraph{AI-READI.}
The AI-READI dataset~\citep{aireadi2024} comprises 896 participants across four clinically defined cohorts (each $n = 224$): Healthy, Pre-T2DM, Oral-medication T2DM, and Insulin-dependent T2DM.
Each participant wore a Dexcom G6 CGM (5-minute sampling) alongside a Fitbit for heart rate and step counts ($\sim$1-minute sampling) for approximately 10 days.
We apply 5-minute resampling, linear interpolation for gaps $\leq$15 minutes, and MinMax normalization per channel.
Training/validation/test splits are user-wise (70/15/15\%).

\paragraph{Loop (T1DM).}
The Loop observational study (JAEB Center for Health Research) provides CGM and insulin pump data from $\sim$1000 participants with Type~1 diabetes using the open-source DIY Loop automated insulin delivery system.
Data includes glucose, basal rate, bolus insulin, and carbohydrate entries, processed through our pipeline's \texttt{loop} format converter.
Loop data exhibits substantially higher glycemic variability than AI-READI cohorts and provides the insulin and carbohydrate covariates required by the pump configuration.

\paragraph{Joined benchmark.}
For the pump and JEPA configurations, we construct a joined dataset (\texttt{loop\_ai\_ready\_joined2.csv}, 12.1M rows) vertically stacking AI-READI (49.9\%, wearable schema) and Loop pump data (50.1\%, T1DM with basal/bolus/carbs).
Sequence IDs are prefixed to avoid collisions, and T1DM row mass is balanced against combined non-T1DM mass.

\paragraph{Personalization subjects.}
Fifteen subjects spanning five clinical groups: 7 T1DM (including one personal CGM/pump user with 345 days of data), and 8 AI-READI subjects (2 per non-T1DM group, $\sim$6 days each).

\subsection{Baselines}

We compare against three baselines, all run on the same data splits:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{N-HiTS}~\citep{challu2023nhits}: neural hierarchical interpolation model from the NeuralForecast library~\citep{neuralforecast2022}, representing a strong non-transformer baseline.
    \item \textbf{GluFormer}~\citep{sergazinov2023gluformer}: transformer with hierarchical attention and uncertainty quantification.
    \item \textbf{Original GluMind}~\citep{bian2025glumind}: the published results from the original paper (same architecture, different training configuration).
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Metrics}

We report three standard metrics: Mean Absolute Error (MAE, mg/dL), Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE, mg/dL), and Mean Absolute Relative Difference (MARD, \%).
MARD is the clinically preferred metric as it accounts for the difference between errors at low versus high glucose values; CGM sensors themselves have $\sim$10\% MARD~\citep{rodbard2016cgm}.

\subsection{Data preprocessing pipeline}

All CGM data is processed through a modular open-source pipeline (\texttt{glucose\_data\_processing}) with 9 device- and study-specific format converters: AI-READI, Loop, HUPA, T1D-UoM, UC-HT (T1DM + healthy controls), Minidose~1, Dexcom~G6, FreeStyle~Libre~3, and Medtronic.
These converters cover the datasets we use most frequently, including multi-modality studies (CGM with heart rate, steps, insulin, carbohydrates, and meals).
The pipeline performs: (1) format-specific consolidation to a standardized schema, (2) gap detection and sequence splitting, (3) linear interpolation for continuous fields with preservation of sparse event signals, (4) fixed-frequency resampling to 5 minutes, and (5) ML-ready export with train/val/test split columns.
We have catalogued 51 public CGM datasets with download links; programmatic downloaders are included for datasets with open access.

% ================================================================
\section{Results}
% ================================================================

\subsection{Overall performance: AI-READI (T2DM spectrum)}

Table~\ref{tab:overall_ai_ready} presents the main results on the AI-READI dataset across four T2DM-spectrum cohorts.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Overall 60-minute glucose prediction on AI-READI (4 cohorts, 896 participants). Lower is better.}
\label{tab:overall_ai_ready}
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Model} & \textbf{MAE} $\downarrow$ & \textbf{RMSE} $\downarrow$ & \textbf{MARD} $\downarrow$ \\
\midrule
\textbf{Sugar~I (ours)} & \textbf{11.33} & \textbf{17.73} & \textbf{8.25\%} \\
GluMind original~\citep{bian2025glumind} & 12.95 & 18.19 & --- \\
GluFormer~\citep{sergazinov2023gluformer} & 19.53 & 33.28 & 13.03\% \\
N-HiTS~\citep{challu2023nhits} & 20.60 & 34.45 & 13.33\% \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Sugar~I outperforms N-HiTS by 45.0\% on MAE, GluFormer by 42.0\%, and the original GluMind implementation by 12.5\%.
The prediction MARD of 8.25\% is below the typical measurement MARD of CGM sensors ($\sim$10\%), meaning the 60-minute \emph{predictions} approach the accuracy of the \emph{sensor itself}.

\subsection{Type~1 diabetes}

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{60-minute glucose prediction on T1DM data. Sugar~I leads on MAE and MARD; N-HiTS retains lower RMSE.}
\label{tab:t1dm}
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Model} & \textbf{MAE} $\downarrow$ & \textbf{RMSE} $\downarrow$ & \textbf{MARD} $\downarrow$ \\
\midrule
\textbf{Sugar~I (ours)} & \textbf{14.51} & 23.00 & \textbf{10.99\%} \\
N-HiTS & 15.11 & \textbf{21.05} & 11.24\% \\
GluFormer & 19.53 & 33.28 & 15.10\% \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

T1DM is inherently harder to predict than T2DM: patients experience large, rapid glucose excursions driven by exogenous insulin timing and carbohydrate intake, producing substantially higher glycemic variability.
Accordingly, all models show higher error on this cohort.
Sugar~I leads on MAE ($-4.0\%$ vs.\ N-HiTS) and MARD, but N-HiTS retains lower RMSE (21.05 vs.\ 23.00), suggesting Sugar~I occasionally produces larger errors on extreme swings---an area for improvement.

\subsection{Error gradient across disease severity}

A consistent finding across all models is that prediction error increases monotonically with disease severity.
Table~\ref{tab:per_cohort} and Figure~\ref{fig:per_cohort_mae} show per-cohort MAE for the combined AI-READI + T1DM benchmark.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Per-cohort MAE (mg/dL) on the combined benchmark. Error tracks disease progression.}
\label{tab:per_cohort}
\begin{tabular}{lccccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Model} & \textbf{Healthy} & \textbf{Pre-T2DM} & \textbf{Oral} & \textbf{Ins-T2DM} & \textbf{T1DM} \\
\midrule
\textbf{Sugar~I} & \textbf{9.57} & \textbf{9.89} & \textbf{12.35} & \textbf{13.58} & \textbf{15.06} \\
N-HiTS & 16.86 & 14.00 & 19.97 & 28.31 & 15.53 \\
GluFormer & 17.08 & 14.21 & 19.36 & 26.36 & 15.46 \\
GluMind orig. & 10.58 & 11.08 & 13.74 & 16.41 & --- \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Sugar~I achieves the largest improvement in the Insulin-T2DM cohort, reducing MAE by $\sim$50\% relative to baselines (13.58 vs.\ 26--28~mg/dL).
This is where glucose dynamics are most complex and multimodal fusion provides the greatest benefit---cross-attention to HR and steps captures cardiovascular correlates of glycemic variability that glucose history alone misses.

\begin{figure}[ht]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.9\textwidth]{fig_per_cohort_mae.png}
\caption{Per-cohort MAE comparison across all models. Sugar~I (teal) maintains consistently low error across the disease spectrum, while baseline models degrade sharply at the Insulin-T2DM stage. The gap narrows for T1DM, where all models face high glycemic variability.}
\label{fig:per_cohort_mae}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Insulin covariate contribution}

On the joined benchmark (12.1M rows, 50\% T1DM from Loop pump data), Sugar~I with full insulin/carb covariates achieves MAE 12.40~mg/dL (Table~\ref{tab:sugarone}).

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Sugar~I (pump) on the joined AI-READI + Loop benchmark. Covariate ablation via zeroing after imputation.}
\label{tab:sugarone}
\begin{tabular}{llccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Configuration} & \textbf{Covariates} & \textbf{MAE} $\downarrow$ & \textbf{RMSE} $\downarrow$ & \textbf{MARD} $\downarrow$ \\
\midrule
\textbf{Sugar~I (pump)} & basal + bolus + carbs & \textbf{12.40} & \textbf{19.03} & \textbf{9.91\%} \\
Sugar~I (pump) & glucose only (\texttt{--zero-cov}) & 12.63 & 19.47 & 9.98\% \\
Sugar~I (wearable) & glucose only & 12.73 & 19.66 & 10.28\% \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

A detailed T1DM covariate ablation (819K windows, 9 users) reveals that \textbf{bolus insulin is the dominant channel}: including bolus alone recovers $\sim$70\% of the gain from using all three covariates (MAE 13.22 vs.\ 13.08 with all, 13.55 with none).
Basal rate contributes least marginally (+0.03~mg/dL when excluded), consistent with its continuous, redundant nature after forward-fill imputation.

\subsection{Personalization}

Fine-tuning the global Sugar~I (pump) checkpoint on individual data produces consistent improvements for T1DM subjects with sufficient history (Table~\ref{tab:personalization}).
Averaged over six T1DM users with $\geq$60 train days:

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Mean test-MAE improvement vs.\ zero-shot, averaged over 6 T1DM users ($\geq$60 train days).}
\label{tab:personalization}
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\toprule
\textbf{Train budget} & \textbf{Mean $\Delta$ MAE} & \textbf{Improvement} \\
\midrule
30 days & $-0.26$ & 0.26 mg/dL \\
60 days & $-0.71$ & 0.71 mg/dL \\
Full train (85--345 d) & $-1.16$ & 1.16 mg/dL \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Gains emerge after 30--60 days; 1--14 day budgets often show flat or slightly worse performance (Figure~\ref{fig:personalization}).
This has direct implications for healthy and prediabetic users who wear a CGM sensor briefly for metabolic optimization: with only 1--2 weeks of data, the global foundation model remains the better option, and personalization should be gated on data availability.
LwF distillation against the global teacher does \emph{not} rescue short-history fine-tunes.
AI-READI subjects ($\sim$6 days of CGM, no insulin covariates) show small and mixed effects, confirming that the short data window typical of health-optimization use is insufficient for fine-tuning.
The best individual improvement was $-2.09$~mg/dL (User 1029, 136 train days).

\begin{figure}[ht]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.9\textwidth]{fig_personalization_curves.png}
\caption{Personalization learning curves for T1DM subjects (first 60 train days). Solid lines show fine-tuned test MAE as a function of personal training days; dashed lines show each user's zero-shot baseline. Most users require 30--60 days before fine-tuning consistently beats the global model.}
\label{fig:personalization}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Continual learning}

Global training outperforms continual (cohort-sequential) training by only 0.14~mg/dL MAE (11.34 vs.\ 11.48), demonstrating that LwF with $\lambda = 0.3$ effectively preserves knowledge across cohorts.
This is relevant for clinical deployment where new patient populations arrive incrementally.

\subsection{Sugar~I + JEPA results}
\label{sec:jepa_results}

\subsubsection{Encoder quality}

Table~\ref{tab:jepa_qc} reports latent-space quality metrics for the five encoder variants.
The 3-day encoder (jepa-864) achieves the highest effective rank (35.99), indicating the best utilization of the 96-dimensional latent space.
Effective rank grows approximately linearly with context window size up to 3 days, but collapses for the 7-day encoder (jepa-2016, rank 16.71).
The 7-day encoder compensates with higher mean standard deviation (0.83 vs.\ 0.56), but this reflects a highly unbalanced distribution: a few leading dimensions dominate while the rest collapse (Figure~\ref{fig:jepa_qc}).

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{JEPA encoder quality metrics. Effective rank measures how many latent dimensions are actively used; mean std measures average spread. Higher is better for both.}
\label{tab:jepa_qc}
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\toprule
\textbf{Encoder} & \textbf{Effective rank} & \textbf{Mean std} \\
\midrule
jepa-128-64 & 17.62 & 0.56 \\
jepa-128 & 26.26 & 0.57 \\
jepa-288 & 27.43 & 0.56 \\
jepa-864 & \textbf{35.99} & 0.56 \\
jepa-2016 & 16.71 & \textbf{0.83} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\begin{figure}[ht]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.48\textwidth]{jepa-864-encoder.png}
\hfill
\includegraphics[width=0.48\textwidth]{jepa-2016-encoder.png}
\caption{JEPA encoder comparison. 3-day encoder (left) shows higher effective rank and more even per-dimension standard deviation. 7-day encoder (right) concentrates variance in a few leading dimensions. Lower plots show PCA variance explained.}
\label{fig:jepa_qc}
\end{figure}

All encoders produce embeddings that clearly separate the AI-READI and Loop datasets along PC1 (Figure~\ref{fig:pca_864}), reflecting their distinct patient populations and sampling densities.
The 3-day encoder shows the strongest separation, yet overall representations remain well-mixed with a low dataset-level silhouette coefficient of 0.166 across all 96 dimensions.

\begin{figure}[ht]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.8\textwidth]{pca_w864.png}
\caption{PCA of jepa-864 embeddings on held-out data. PC1 separates AI-READI from Loop, but the overall representation is well-mixed (silhouette = 0.166).}
\label{fig:pca_864}
\end{figure}

\subsubsection{Patient identification probing}

Linear probing on frozen encoders for patient identification (Table~\ref{tab:jepa_patient}) reveals that the 3-day encoder reaches 68.4\% validation accuracy across 665 previously unseen patients.
Accuracy correlates with context window size; the 7-day encoder cannot be reliably evaluated due to insufficient patients ($n = 18$) with 7 consecutive days of measurements.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Patient identification from frozen JEPA embeddings. $^{*}$jepa-2016 evaluated on 18 patients; all others on 665.}
\label{tab:jepa_patient}
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Encoder} & \textbf{Train Loss} & \textbf{Train Acc.} & \textbf{Val Loss} & \textbf{Val Acc.} \\
\midrule
jepa-128 & 4.09 & 0.136 & 4.41 & 0.113 \\
jepa-288 & 2.24 & 0.419 & 2.59 & 0.365 \\
\textbf{jepa-864} & \textbf{0.69} & \textbf{0.816} & \textbf{0.96} & \textbf{0.684} \\
jepa-2016$^{*}$ & 1.23 & 0.583 & 1.71 & 0.483 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

These results confirm that JEPA embeddings encode substantial patient-specific information, supporting their potential as patient embeddings for zero-shot personalization.

\subsubsection{Sugar~I + JEPA forecasting performance}

Table~\ref{tab:sugar_jepa_test} presents Sugar~I + JEPA test metrics across all encoder variants and the original CGM-JEPA encoder for comparison.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Sugar~I + JEPA test metrics on the joined benchmark. All encoders improve over the pump-only baseline (MAE 12.40).}
\label{tab:sugar_jepa_test}
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Encoder} & \textbf{MAE} $\downarrow$ & \textbf{RMSE} $\downarrow$ & \textbf{MARD} $\downarrow$ \\
\midrule
jepa-128-64 & 12.47 & 18.82 & 10.40\% \\
jepa-128 & 12.03 & 18.54 & 9.80\% \\
jepa-288 & 11.37 & 17.63 & 9.08\% \\
jepa-864 & 10.90 & 17.06 & 8.55\% \\
\textbf{jepa-2016} & \textbf{10.40} & \textbf{16.19} & \textbf{8.10\%} \\
CGM-JEPA~\citep{cgmjepa2025} & 11.41 & 17.92 & 8.99\% \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

JEPA integration is consistently beneficial, with gains increasing alongside context window size.
The 1-day encoder (jepa-288) already reaches average MARD of 9.08\%---approaching the $\sim$9--10\% threshold where CGM sensor noise itself becomes the dominant error source~\citep{garg2022dexcomg7}.
The 3-day encoder (jepa-864) achieves 14\% MARD improvement over the pump-only baseline while requiring less than half the lookback data of the 7-day variant.
The 7-day encoder (jepa-2016) pushes MAE to 10.40 (18\% improvement, MARD 8.10\%), but with diminishing returns relative to the data requirement.
The 96-dimension embedding consistently outperforms the 64-dimension variant (jepa-128 vs.\ jepa-128-64).
Our retrained encoders outperform the original CGM-JEPA encoder (MAE 11.41) at every context length from 1 day onward, likely due to the larger training corpus and VICReg regularization.

% ================================================================
\section{Discussion}
% ================================================================

\paragraph{Parallel attention as architectural principle.}
The key architectural difference between Sugar~I and prior work (e.g., AttenGluco) is the \emph{parallel} execution of cross-attention and multi-scale self-attention within each block.
Where AttenGluco applies these sequentially, the parallel design allows each mechanism to specialize: cross-attention handles multimodal fusion while multi-scale attention captures temporal patterns at different resolutions.
Their outputs are combined by summation and LayerNorm, enabling the model to jointly reason about ``which sensor matters when'' and ``what temporal scale is relevant.''

\paragraph{Error gradient reflects disease physiology.}
The monotonic increase in prediction error from Healthy to T1DM (Table~\ref{tab:per_cohort}) directly reflects increasing glycemic variability with disease progression.
T1DM and insulin-dependent T2DM patients exhibit the largest and most abrupt glucose excursions, driven by exogenous insulin timing and meal composition---signals that are only partially observable from CGM and wearable data alone.
This error gradient is consistent across all tested models and is an expected consequence of the underlying physiology, not a modeling limitation: prediction difficulty is an intrinsic property of the clinical phenotype.

\paragraph{Learnable mixing for heterogeneous covariates.}
The learnable softmax mixing (Eq.~3) is a simple but effective solution for combining covariates with very different signal characteristics.
Bolus insulin is sparse (present on only 4\% of rows) but highly informative; basal rate is dense but largely redundant with glucose trend after imputation.
Fixed averaging would under-weight bolus and over-weight basal; the learned weights adapt automatically.

\paragraph{Personalization: foundation model meets minimal data.}
We frame personalization as fine-tuning a foundation model (the global Sugar~I checkpoint) on individual data, and ask what the minimum viable data budget is.
For T1DM subjects with $\geq$60 days of history, fine-tuning consistently improves prediction (average $-0.71$~mg/dL MAE).
However, short data budgets (1--14 days) can be harmful---a crucial finding for healthy and prediabetic users who typically wear a CGM sensor for only one or two weeks per year for metabolic health optimization.
For these users, the global model currently remains the better option.
The critical technical finding is that global-checkpoint scalers must be reused rather than refitted: personal data shifts the input distribution away from the pretrained model, causing short fine-tunes to degrade.
For deployment, a practical gate should skip personalization when insufficient data is available, and the JEPA-based patient embedding (Section~\ref{sec:jepa_results}) offers a potential path to zero-shot personalization that does not require accumulating weeks of data.

\paragraph{JEPA context window: quality versus data cost.}
Encoder quality (effective rank) and forecasting accuracy both improve with longer context windows up to 3 days, but diverge beyond that.
The 7-day encoder collapses to effective rank 16.71 (vs.\ 35.99 for 3-day), concentrating variance in a few dimensions---yet still achieves the best downstream MAE (10.40).
This suggests that even a partially degenerate representation can carry predictive information when the raw lookback is long enough, but the 3-day encoder offers the best quality/data tradeoff: 14\% MARD improvement over baseline while requiring only 3 days of lookback versus 7.

\paragraph{JEPA as zero-shot patient embedding.}
The 68\% patient identification accuracy from frozen 3-day JEPA embeddings (665 patients, linear probe only) demonstrates that JEPA representations capture substantial patient-specific metabolic signatures.
This opens a path toward zero-shot personalization: rather than accumulating 60+ days of data for fine-tuning, a 3-day JEPA observation window could provide implicit patient context through the cross-attention stream.
While we have not yet evaluated this in a prospective setting, the patient recognition results provide encouraging evidence.

\paragraph{Limitations.}
(1) Sugar~I's RMSE on T1DM exceeds N-HiTS (23.00 vs.\ 21.05), indicating occasional large errors on extreme glucose swings.
(2) The covariate ablation is inference-time only (same trained weights, zeroed inputs); retraining with matched covariate subsets could change relative rankings.
(3) The 60-minute prediction horizon, while clinically actionable, is shorter than the 2--4 hour horizons explored by some models.
(4) Sugar-Sugar results are not yet available (study currently recruiting).
(5) Sugar~I + JEPA fine-tuning on individual patients has not yet been evaluated.
(6) Better self-supervised training methods may be available, e.g., Sketched Isotropic Gaussian Regularization (SigReg).

\paragraph{Generalizability.}
The parallel cross-attention + multi-scale design is not glucose-specific.
It applies to any setting where multiple physiological signals arrive at different sampling rates and carry complementary information: ECG + SpO$_2$ for cardiac monitoring, EEG + EMG for sleep staging, or cortisol + HR + activity for stress prediction.
The architecture is a recipe for multimodal biomedical time-series fusion, not a one-off glucose solution.

% ================================================================
\section{Conclusion}
% ================================================================

We presented Sugar~I, a parallel-attention transformer achieving 8.25\% MARD on 60-minute glucose prediction across the T2DM spectrum---approaching CGM sensor accuracy and outperforming existing models by 40--45\%.
The pump configuration demonstrates that learnable softmax mixing effectively integrates sparse insulin signals with continuous glucose history.
The JEPA hybrid further improves prediction by incorporating self-supervised representations as an auxiliary cross-attention stream, achieving MAE 10.40~mg/dL (MARD 8.10\%) with a 7-day encoder---an 18\% improvement over the pump-only baseline.
JEPA embeddings also encode patient-specific information (68\% identification accuracy), opening a path toward zero-shot personalization that does not require weeks of data collection.
Personalization via fine-tuning improves predictions for individuals with sufficient personal CGM history ($\geq$60 days).
The ongoing Sugar-Sugar study will provide the first human accuracy reference for this task, enabling the field to move beyond model-versus-model comparisons.
All code and model weights are available at \url{https://github.com/GlucoseDAO}.

